schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Review and Maturity: Labelling What Your Knowledge Can Be Trusted To Do"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: framework
  summary: >-
    Guidance for labelling how trustworthy each page in a knowledge commons
    is, so trust survives founder/author turnover. Names the trap ("polished
    writing is not automatically reviewed knowledge"), borrows three proven
    maturity ladders (Wikipedia content assessment, ADR/MADR status field,
    Kubernetes Alpha/Beta/Stable), proposes a shippable four-rung ladder
    (Draft/Reviewed/Maintained/Deprecated) stored in frontmatter, ties review
    rigor to risk level, and treats maturity as an ongoing maintenance signal
    (expiry by date, compost instead of delete) rather than a one-time label.
  audience: small-team leads building docs/wikis that must outlive their authors
  known_tensions:
    - "self-claimed states (draft/proposed/alpha) vs. granted states (reviewed/accepted/stable) — the promotion gate only belongs on granted tiers"
    - "review-everything-equally burns teams out vs. review-scaled-to-risk leaves gaps if risk is misjudged"
  related_concepts:
    - review-maturity-ladder
    - bus-factor
    - silent-drift
  related_resources:
    - MADR (Markdown Architectural Decision Records)
    - Diátaxis
    - "Wikipedia: Content assessment"
    - "Kubernetes: Feature Gates"
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/review-and-maturity.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

